  (d) params_init_param_container/build API reshaped [DONE, done as a
  follow-up right after (a)-(c) landed, same session]
  The batch, struct-of-parallel-arrays signature this section originally
  described (num_of_params + 7 parallel arrays in one call) turned out to
  be worth splitting further, immediately after building it: params_init_
  param_container(user_data_per_container) now creates an EMPTY container,
  and a new int param_add_param(container, name, val, min, max, inc, uid,
  cookie) appends one parameter at a time, returning its val_id (-1 on a
  duplicate uid or allocation failure). uid uniqueness is checked per-add
  via param_find_uid (already built for (c)) - the old batch params_uids_
  have_duplicate scan is gone, folded into that one check.
  Why this is more than a style preference: clap_plugins.c and plugins.c
  (LV2) discover their param count and values at RUNTIME, so the old batch
  API forced them to calloc 7 temp arrays, fill them in a loop, make one
  call, then free everything - including heap-allocating every param NAME
  up front (calloc+snprintf for CLAP, strdup for LV2) purely so all N names
  could stay alive simultaneously until the one batch call. With param_add_
  param, a name only needs to survive one synchronous call - params.c
  already copies it into PRM_PARAM.name[] immediately - so CLAP's param_
  info.name and LV2's lilv_node_as_string() now pass straight through with
  no heap allocation and no matching free loop at all. Net allocation count
  for a dynamically-discovered param set went down, not up - directly
  serves STAGE 2's already-stated "reduce allocation count" goal, not a
  detour from it.
  Also serves STAGE 2 directly: params_container_resync's own "add a new
  param" step (the pass-2 half of the diff-and-mutate algorithm designed
  above) needs exactly this primitive - append one param to a container
  that may already have others in it. param_add_param as built now always
  grows by one (no tombstone-reuse yet, since there is no tombstone/alive
  concept without STAGE 2's dynamic-remove support); STAGE 2 extends this
  SAME function to check a freed slot first, rather than writing a second,
  separate add path.
  Synth.c/jack_funcs.c (Trk) benefited too, despite having a fixed,
  compile-time-known param set with no loop to begin with: the old call
  site had 5-7 parallel compound-literal arrays (name[], val[], min[],
  max[], inc[], uid[]) that had to be read down through in lockstep to
  know what any one param even was, and a silent misalignment (inserting a
  param in the middle of one array without updating the others) was a real,
  hard-to-spot correctness hazard. N one-line param_add_param calls, one
  self-contained line per param, make that class of bug structurally
  impossible rather than merely avoided by care - a real readability/safety
  win for the static-set owners too, not just the dynamic-discovery ones.
  One correctness point caught while migrating clap_plugins.c/plugins.c:
  both loops use their own external index (CLAP's param_index, LV2's
  plug->controls index) as this container's val_id too, and other code
  (param_get_value calls in plug_run_rt / clap_plugins.c's RT param-event
  loop) assumes that same index lines up 1:1 between plug->controls/CLAP's
  own numbering and this container's val_id. So a param_id that fails
  get_info (CLAP) or a NULL plug->controls[i] slot (LV2) still gets a
  param_add_param call (with a safe non-NULL placeholder name, e.g. "") to
  keep the index aligned, rather than being skipped - skipping would have
  desynced every param after it. The old array-based code did this
  correctly too (always wrote a default value into that array slot); this
  is calling that out explicitly since it would have been an easy thing to
  silently break while switching to a call-per-param loop.

  (e) PRM_PARAM storage: one malloc per parameter, not one block [DONE,
  decided and built right after (d)]
  rt_params/ui_params changed from PRM_PARAM* (one realloc'd block) to
  PRM_PARAM** (an array of individually malloc'd PRM_PARAMs). param_add_
  param now mallocs one new PRM_PARAM, then grows the OUTER pointer array
  with realloc - safe, because realloc there only ever moves pointer
  VALUES around, never the PRM_PARAMs they point at. param_clean_param_
  container frees each PRM_PARAM individually before freeing the outer
  arrays. Every accessor changed from &(param_array[val_id]) (address of a
  value inside one block) to param_array[val_id] (dereference a stored
  pointer) - mechanical, touched every function in the file, verified with
  a full -Wall -Wextra build (clean) plus a valgrind run through this
  app's actual startup (sampler/synth/LV2/CLAP/Trk all call param_add_param
  during init) - 0 errors, 0 bytes definitely lost.
  Why this reverses part of (d)'s original tradeoff: (d) kept the
  contiguous block specifically to avoid "more allocations" - but once CX
  integration (STAGE 3) is factored in, that concern doesn't hold up.
  Stage 3 will materialise essentially every param as a CX node, and a CX
  node's user_data has to be something that survives time (see the cross-
  cutting invariant) - so *some* per-param allocation was always going to
  be needed once STAGE 3 lands, whether it's the PRM_PARAM itself or a
  smaller handle pointing at one. Between "malloc the param itself, and
  let user_data be that pointer directly - exactly the same pattern
  sample_ops/lv2_plugin_ops/clap_plugin_ops already use" and "keep the
  contiguous block, and teach app_data.c a params-only exception where
  user_data is a {container, uid} handle resolved fresh through param_
  find_uid on every access" - the first keeps every CX-backed module on
  the identical pattern (a raw, cache-once, safe pointer) with zero special
  casing anywhere outside params.c; the second would have been real, ap-
  data.c-side edge-case code for one module, for a savings that stage 3
  spends right back anyway. "Reduce allocation count" (STAGE 2, below) was
  always about *unnecessary* allocation (e.g. giving every param an
  interp_val whether it used one or not) - one malloc to let a parameter
  exist as a distinct, independently-addressable thing isn't in that
  category once the CX layer needs that guarantee regardless.
  Cache-locality cost of scattered PRM_PARAMs checked against this app's
  actual RT access patterns before accepting the tradeoff, not assumed:
  every RT read is a targeted single-index lookup (param_msgs_process's
  prm_array[cur_bit.val_id], synth.c's ten separately-named param_get_value
  calls with hardcoded indices) - there is no sequential "scan all params"
  hot path anywhere in this codebase today. The one place that does
  iterate every param (CLAP's per-cycle changed-value check) does it every
  single audio cycle, which is exactly the case where the working set
  stays cache-resident regardless of layout - contiguity only pays off on
  a cold touch, and this data never goes cold. Net: no measurable RT cost
  from this change given how the code actually reads params, not just "we
  can afford it."

  (f) val_id vs uid - not redundant, different jobs, worth stating plainly
  since the question came up: val_id is a direct O(1) array-position
  handle (what every accessor already takes); uid is a stable, owner-
  chosen identity that costs an O(N) param_find_uid scan to resolve to a
  val_id. val_id is for a caller that already knows where a param lives
  (every hot RT read in this codebase); uid is for cross-time/cross-
  module identity - surviving a resync, or being the thing a ContextId's
  local part is built from (STAGE 3). The relationship mirrors the CX
  layer's own "resolve a ContextId to a fast handle once, reuse the handle
  many times" pattern - val_id IS params.c's own version of that fast
  handle, uid->val_id (via param_find_uid) is the one-time resolve step.

