  (h) PRM_PARAM split into PRM_PARAM_RT / PRM_PARAM_UI [DONE] - the last,
  most invasive piece of stage 1. Forcing question: with rt_params/ui_params
  now two arrays of individually-malloc'd structs (see (e)), is duplicating
  the WHOLE struct on both sides actually necessary, given only .val truly
  needs to cross threads? Audited every real field against every actual
  call site (not guessed) to answer it:
    - RT-only: cookie (only clap_plugins.c's rt-thread event-building path
      ever reads a param's cookie; nothing on the ui side ever has).
    - UI-only: inc_am, def_val, name, is_hidden (Increase/Decrease/DefValue/
      ChangeName/ToggleHidden are all ui-side-only operations - the rt side
      never computes any of them, it only ever receives a final value or
      originates one directly).
    - Both: val, min_val, max_val, uid. val_changed also lives on both
      structs by name but is RT-ONLY in practice - the ui side has nothing
      that polls it (see below).
  Two struct types replaced the one shared PRM_PARAM:
    PRM_PARAM_RT { val, min_val, max_val, val_changed, uid, cookie }
    PRM_PARAM_UI { val, min_val, max_val, def_val, inc_am, name, uid,
                   is_hidden }
  name/ui_name collapsed into UI's single mutable name[] - now that uid (not
  name) is the stable cross-time identity (see (f)), nothing depends on a
  param's display name staying fixed, so ChangeName can just rewrite name[]
  directly. ui_name_just_changed removed entirely, not renamed/kept: once a
  name-changed consumer exists it belongs on the CX layer's own push-based
  DATA_EVENT_CHANGED mechanism (already built, see the actions/CX work),
  which doesn't need a per-param polled flag at all - a stored "did the name
  change" bit would just be duplicating what CX already does better.
  just_changed renamed to val_changed and confined to PRM_PARAM_RT only -
  it exists because CLAP/JACK-transport rt-thread loops poll it every audio
  cycle to decide whether an external protocol still needs telling about a
  value (param_get_if_changed_rt/param_get_if_any_changed_rt); the ui side
  never had an equivalent consumer, so it doesn't get the field.
  Cross-thread protocol changed at the same time, and had to: previously
  param_set_value sent the ORIGINAL operation (param_op + raw set_to) across
  the ring buffer and the receiving side replayed it via param_set_value_
  directly, clamping against ITS OWN min/max. That only worked because both
  sides carried a full PRM_PARAM with min/max (and inc_am/def_val for the ui
  ops). Once PRM_PARAM_RT drops inc_am/def_val, rt can no longer replay
  Increase/Decrease/DefValue - so the protocol changed to "whichever side
  originates a change computes and clamps the FINAL value itself, sends
  just that". PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT (types.h) shrank from {val_id, param_
  value, param_string, param_op} to {val_id, param_value} accordingly - the
  receiving side (param_msgs_process) now just stores the value verbatim,
  no operation/string/clamping logic on that path at all.
  Function surface changed with an explicit, non-arbitrary rule (not
  "split everything" or "split nothing"): a function whose valid operation
  SET differs by side split into an unsuffixed ui function + an _rt
  function (param_set_value / param_set_value_rt, param_cookie_return_rt,
  param_get_if_changed_rt, param_get_if_any_changed_rt); a function that
  does the exact same thing on either side, just reading a different array,
  stayed ONE function taking an rt_params flag (param_get_uid, param_get_
  value, param_return_num_params) - splitting those would have produced
  near-duplicate bodies differing only in which array they read, which is
  exactly the "parallel signature twins" shape this codebase avoids
  elsewhere (see the no-parallel-signature-twins memory). param_find_uid
  lost its rt_params argument entirely and become ui-only, since uid is
  identical on both sides once param_add_param sets it and nothing on the
  rt side has ever needed to resolve one - checking the ui array alone is
  correct and sufficient. param_find_name removed outright (no call site
  anywhere used it - name was never how anything looked a param up, uid/
  val_id always were).
