  (j) clap_plug_params_value_to_text: stop re-deriving param_info.id via a
  fresh get_info call on every string conversion [DONE] - the uid stored in
  the container IS param_info.id already (set once, at creation time, in
  clap_plug_params_create's param_add_param call), so a plugin round-trip
  to re-fetch the same value on every single param_get_value_as_string call
  was pure waste. Replaced with param_get_uid(plug->plug_params, val_id,
  0) - an O(1) container read instead of a call into the plugin.
  This reopened the one edge case get_info's fresh call used to guard
  against for free: a param whose OWN get_info failed at creation time gets
  added with an empty name and its uid set to the fallback loop index
  (see (c)/clap_plug_params_create's own comment), not a real clap_id.
  Under the old code, calling get_info again here would almost certainly
  fail again too and return 0 safely; under the new code, that fallback
  "uid" gets handed straight to value_to_text as if it were a real clap_id
  - which could coincidentally match a DIFFERENT param's actual id and
  silently return the wrong plugin's label instead of failing cleanly.
  Fixed with an explicit guard, not by reverting the optimisation: a
  badly-created param is exactly identifiable by its empty name (the same
  marker clap_plug_params_create itself uses), so clap_plug_params_value_
  to_text now checks param_get_name first and returns 0 (nothing written,
  matching every other "can't format this" path in this function) if the
  name is empty, before ever touching param_get_uid/value_to_text.
  Verified: make, gcc -Wall -Wextra on clap_plugins.c (only pre-existing,
  unrelated warnings), valgrind against the built binary's actual startup
  path - 0 errors, 0 bytes definitely lost.

