    Algorithm [DECIDED] - params_container_resync(container, new_params[],
    new_count), called by the owner (CLAP's RESCAN_ALL handler today, any
    future dynamic-params owner later) with its CURRENT full param list,
    matched by uid not position, per the CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT (tombstone,
    never compact a surviving slot):
      pass 1 - removals: for each currently-alive slot whose uid is no
        longer present in new_params: release any owned state, mark the
        slot dead (tombstoned), do NOT shift anything.
      pass 2 - additions: for each new_params[i] whose uid has no matching
        alive slot: reuse a tombstoned slot if one exists, else grow
        rt_params/ui_params by one (realloc); populate BOTH structs exactly
        like param_add_param already does per-param today [field list
        updated for stage 1(h)'s RT/UI split - the old single-PRM_PARAM
        field list (val_type, just_changed, ...) this line used to name no
        longer exists]: PRM_PARAM_RT gets val, min_val, max_val, val_
        changed=0, uid, cookie; PRM_PARAM_UI gets val, min_val, max_val,
        def_val=val, inc_am, name, uid, is_hidden=0; plus alive=true on
        whichever tombstone-tracking field STAGE 2 ends up adding to both.
      survivors (uid found in both): untouched entirely - not visited by
        either pass. Value/name refresh for survivors is RESCAN_VALUES/
        INFO's job (already implemented, separate from this), not this
        function's.
      Both rt_params and ui_params get identical treatment in lockstep,
      same slot index in both, mirroring params_init_param_container's
      existing symmetry. Safe to mutate both directly (no ring-buffer
      messaging needed for the structural part) because CLAP only allows
      RESCAN_ALL while the plugin instance is deactivated (the existing
      `if (plug->plug_inst_activated == 1) return;` guard already in
      clap_plug_ext_params_rescan enforces this) - the RT thread isn't
      reading rt_params for that plugin while this runs.
      This function does NOT report what changed to anyone - it only
      leaves the container in the correct new state. STAGE 3's CX resync
      independently discovers what actually changed later, by re-walking
      child_at and diffing against what it already has - same as it
      already does for every other container. Two separate diffs (this
