STAGE 3 - parameters as contexts (CX), integrated into the action system
  Agreed direction: yes, parameters should be CX tree nodes, not a live
  external list like JACK ports. The reasoning that settled ports as
  NOT contexts was that they are externally driven and churny (hotplug,
  processes outside this program). Parameters are the opposite - owned and
  defined entirely by this app's own modules, stable for the lifetime of
  their owning sample/plugin instance/synth osc, exactly like the loaded
  plugin instances that already are CX children. So parameters should be
  browsable/actionable the same way samples and plugins already are.
  Open questions to settle before building (not decided):
  - Materialize every parameter as a CX child of its owning context, or
    only non-hidden ones (PRM_PARAM.is_hidden already exists)? A plugin can
    have a lot of params (tens to low hundreds) - worth checking real
    numbers against how the rest of the CX tree scales before committing.
  - Value changes go through the ui-side param_set_value and its ring-
    buffer message to the rt thread - this already matches the pattern the
    rest of the app uses (JACK, plugins), shouldn't need new plumbing, just
    confirm during implementation. [Signature note: param_set_value no
    longer takes an rt_params argument as of stage 1(h) - it's
    unconditionally the ui-side function now; param_set_value_rt is the
    separate rt-originated function and isn't relevant to action_do, since
    actions always originate ui-side.]
  - How params become CX children, and how structural changes (RESCAN_ALL)
    reach app_intrf [DECIDED - reuses the existing generic pipeline,
    verified against actual code, not assumed]:
      app_intrf's resync (enumerate a parent's current children via its
      ops table, diff against the CX tree, emit CX_ADDED/CX_REMOVED) is
      ALREADY fully generic - it doesn't know or care what kind of child
      it's looking at. It needs ZERO new code for params. The "structural
      change happened" signal is NOT a new DataEvent type either - it's
      the exact same DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED already used for "a
      plugin was added to the plugins list", just now ALSO fired targeting
      a plugin/synth-osc/sample INSTANCE's own ContextId (not only
      container-list ContextIds).
      What's actually new, checked directly against app_data.c: every
      item-level ops table - sample_ops, lv2_plugin_ops, clap_plugin_ops,
      the synth osc's ops - currently stubs child_at to unconditionally
      `return false;` (`(void)user_data; (void)idx; (void)out; return
      false;`). They are hardcoded leaves today, not "almost wired up".
      Giving them real param children means implementing REAL child_at/
      child_count there, backed by PRM_CONTAIN (once STAGE 2 lands).
      Each owning instance needs its own params_dirty bool (NOT the
      existing container-level plugins_dirty/samples_dirty - those mean
      "the SET of loaded instances changed"; params_dirty is instance-
      scoped, since one plugin's RESCAN_ALL says nothing about another's),
      set by the RESCAN_ALL handler right after params_container_resync
      finishes (mutate first, flag second).
      That flag doesn't need a new iteration to check it either -
      clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages (already called every cycle from
      app_data_update) already loops every currently-loaded CLAP_PLUG_PLUG
      to drain each instance's param_msgs_process. Checking
      cur_plug->params_dirty and pushing DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED for
      that instance's ContextId slots into that same existing loop - one
      more check per iteration, not a second pass. (Needs to loop, not be
      a single flag check like JACK's ports_changed - several loaded
      plugins could rescan in the same cycle, unlike JACK which is one
      client.) LV2/synth/sampler would need the equivalent read function
      to grow the same kind of check, whenever/if any of them gain a
      dynamic-params story of their own.
