    Challenged and RE-CONFIRMED [DECIDED, stays SET_CHOICE/list_at, not
    UI-side enumeration]: question raised was whether SET_CHOICE leaks
    param functionality onto the UI, versus an alternative where an enum
    param just stays an ordinary SET_VALUE/ADJUST_VALUE param (inc=1,
    min/max = the enum's range) and the UI itself loops min..max calling a
    value-accepting param_get_value_as_string to build its own picker,
    needing no new DataActionType/list_at at all.
    Verdict: keep SET_CHOICE/list_at as the mechanism - the alternative
    inverts where the leak actually is. If the UI has to know "this param's
    domain is a walkable, gap-free integer range, so step it and treat each
    string as a label", that IS the param owning UI reconstructing
    domain-specific behaviour from raw metadata - the exact pattern already
    rejected for ADJUST_VALUE's step semantics earlier in this same
    section ("what one step means is owner-defined behaviour... not
    something the UI should reconstruct from raw metadata"). SET_CHOICE/
    list_at keeps that loop in ONE place (the data layer, written once per
    owner) and hands the UI an already-built flat list - the identical
    shape ADD_CHOICE already uses, so helper_choice_list_step needs zero
    new code. Without it, every future UI implementation (not just the
    CLI) would need its own copy of "detect enum-shaped param, loop
    min..max, build picker" - the duplication SET_CHOICE avoids.
    The list_at implementation itself is cheap, not a new subsystem: CLAP's
    own IS_ENUM contract guarantees a contiguous, gap-free integer range
    (spec: must be IS_STEPPED, no blank value_to_text() anywhere in
    min..max), and clap_plugins.c already forces inc=1.0 for any
    IS_STEPPED param (clap_plug_params_create, the CLAP_PARAM_IS_STEPPED
    check) - so a param's own list_at is just `for(v=min; v<=max; v+=1.0)`
    calling param_get_value_as_string(container, val_id, v, ...) per step,
    which is exactly the primitive the next paragraph generalises.
