  - Param categories [DECIDED: Path A - flat CX children, category is
    metadata, not a tree level]. CLAP already gives a real input for this -
    clap_param_info_t.module (verified against the vendored header,
    /usr/include/clap/ext/params.h): "The module path containing the
    param, eg: 'Oscillators/Wavetable 1'. '/' will be used as a separator
    to show a tree-like structure." Two ways to use it were weighed:
      Path A (chosen): PRM_PARAM gets a category/module string field.
        Params stay flat, direct CX children of their owning instance,
        exactly as already designed above - category is presentational
        metadata only, not a tree level. Some accessor needs to expose it
        to a consumer (extend name()'s output, or a small dedicated op) -
        one shared, one-time addition, not per-UI cost.
      Path B (rejected for now): each distinct module path becomes its
        own real intermediate CX node (params become ITS children instead
        of the instance's). Rejected because:
        - child_at only ever receives a single void *user_data, with no
          side channel for "which subset" the way list_at gets partial -
          so a category node needs its own genuinely separate, persisted,
          stable-identity storage (its own small per-instance tombstoned
          array, uid = hash of the full path) to exist at all. The flag-
          plus-scope trick built for CHOICE-list categories (see the
          FUTURE block below) does NOT transfer here - it specifically
          exploited partial being threaded through list_at's contract,
          which child_at/the plain CX tree contract does not have.
        - CLAP lists "module change" as one of the reasons RESCAN_INFO
          fires - a param's category can change at runtime without the
          param itself being added/removed. Under Path B this means the
          param's CX node needs a different PARENT, and CX parentage is
          fixed at creation (app_intrf_cx_create takes parent_cx once,
          no reparent primitive exists) - so a mere category relabel
          would need to be handled as remove-and-recreate at the CX
          layer, even though the param's own identity/value never
          changed. Under Path A it is just updating a string field on
          the already-existing struct - no CX event needed beyond an
          optional presentational DATA_EVENT_CHANGED if a UI wants to
          refresh its display of the grouping.
      Path A is upgradable to Path B later without waste if it turns out
      to be needed: the one thing Path B would need as input - each
      param's category/module path - is exactly what Path A already
      stores for its own display purposes, and param ContextIds (uid-
      derived) do not reference their parent, so they are unaffected by
      which path is chosen.
      Cost of Path A: hjkl navigation through a flat list of all of a
      plugin's params is more keypresses to reach one that is far away,
      versus being able to drill straight into a smaller subset - cheaply
      softened by having child_at return params sorted by category (same-
      category params land adjacent even though still flat/direct
      children), so a UI can at least print a header as it scrolls past a
      contiguous run without needing a real tree node for it.
      Path B's one real advantage over Path A is exactly that - shorter
      navigation to reach a specific category's params - not anything
      about actions, identity, or staleness, which are identical either
      way. Worth checking real per-plugin param counts before assuming
      this cost actually matters in practice (same open caveat already
      noted above for "materialise every param as a CX child").
      Stress-tested against a hypothetical richer UI (collapsed
      categories, expand with l) before deciding this was safe to commit
      to - confirmed it needs zero data-layer changes either way, only
      where the UI-side cost lands differs by interaction style:
        - literal inline expand/collapse within one continuous scrolling
          list (synthetic header rows interleaved with real param rows,
          toggled in place) is the more expensive one to build under
          Path A, since it needs its own client-side derived view-model -
          group the flat children by category once, keep a per-category
          expanded bool as pure UI state (never persisted data-side,
          rebuilt whenever the user navigates to a different instance),
          rebuild an ordered "display rows" list on every toggle, and
          move the cursor through that derived list instead of directly
          through child_at's indices. Path B gets this exact behaviour
          for free from ordinary tree navigation instead (a node's
          children are already invisible until you enter it).
        - drilling (a small category-list view; l switches to a filtered
          param-list view scoped to that category; h/ESC switches back)
          is cheap under Path A - it is the same shape as
          helper_action_do_connect's existing SOURCE/TARGETS two-mode
          navigator: group params by category client-side once, a mode
          (CATEGORY_LIST | PARAM_LIST) plus two cursors, l on a category
          switches mode and resets the param cursor, h/ESC in PARAM_LIST
          mode switches back with the category cursor preserved. Bounded,
          already-proven pattern, not new complexity - and arguably the
          better fit for a keyboard-driven CLI than inline expansion
          would be anyway.
      Either interaction style is entirely UI-side work on top of the
      same flat child_at results - confirms Path A does not box out a
      richer future UI, it just leaves that UI's presentation complexity
      where it belongs, in that UI, rather than in the data layer.

================================================================================
FUTURE - categories for CHOICE / MULTI_CHOICE lists (design sketch, not
scheduled - written down now because plugin presets will need it: preset
browsers are essentially always categorised, e.g. LV2 preset banks/CLAP
preset factories exposing a category/collection hierarchy)
================================================================================
  No new DataListId / DataArgKind / DataActionType needed - a category-
  bearing list is still just a flat, idx-enumerable list_at, exactly like
  today. Two additions to data_actions.h cover it:
  - DataChoice gains a second flags bit: DATA_CHOICE_CATEGORY (1u << 1),
    alongside the existing DATA_CHOICE_LINKED (1u << 0). A row with this bit
    set is a node to descend into, not a pickable leaf - same idea as a
    directory listing mixing files and subdirectories in one flat readdir,
    "is this a directory" is a flag on the entry, not a different call.
  - DataActionReq gains a top-level field, sibling to `type`, OUTSIDE the
    action-specific union: `uint64_t category_scope;` (MAKE_ID(list_ns,
    category key), 0 = list root). Set by the UI while drilling a
    category-bearing list; ignored by list_at for lists that have no
    categories; never meaningful in the req that finally reaches action_do
    - only the union fields (add_choice/connect/...) are, so no ops table's
    action_do needs to change for this to exist.
    [Correction from an earlier version of this idea: originally proposed
    reusing add_choice.choice_value itself as the drill scope while
    browsing (elegant for ADD_CHOICE's single list, since nothing else
    needs that field mid-browse) - doesn't generalise. CONNECT's targets
    list is already scoped by partial->connect.source, which has to stay
    the fixed, correct source for the whole browsing session; a second,
    independent "which category am I inside" value has no spare field to
    live in without colliding with source. A dedicated top-level field
    avoids the collision and works identically for every action shape.]

  How drilling works, CLI side: one more branch in the existing "on
  SELECTED" handling already used by helper_choice_list_step's callers, not
  a new architecture -
    - pick-and-return shape (CONNECT's SOURCE mode, a plain non-repeating
      CHOICE): category row -> update category_scope, reset cursor, browse
      the same list again; leaf row -> return, exactly as today.
    - act-and-continue shape (ADD_CHOICE's repeat, CONNECT's TARGETS
      toggle): category row -> descend, same as above; leaf row -> act on
      it (execute / toggle) and keep looping at the SAME scope, exactly as
      today.
  Backing out (h/ESC) needs a small caller-owned breadcrumb: a fixed-size
  stack of (scope value, cursor) pairs, one per level drilled into, capped
  like ACTION_ARG_COUNT/MAX_ACTION_CANDIDATES already are elsewhere in this
  CLI - no heap. Pop one level per h/ESC (cursor restored, same as CONNECT's
  source_cursor preservation) until back at the root, where it behaves like
  today (leaves the picker / steps back to the outer mode).

  Two things list_at implementations need to get right, whenever this is
  actually built:
  - A category node's own DataChoice.value must hash its FULL PATH (e.g.
    fnv1a64("Bass/Analog/Warm")), not just its own name - otherwise two
    different parents with a same-named child (Bass/Warm vs Lead/Warm)
    collide. Leaf items keep using whatever globally-unique identity they
    already have (a preset URI/path) - category_scope only filters which
    leaves are visible at a given level, it is never folded into a leaf's
    own identity.
  - Only the immediate parent's scope value is ever needed by list_at (not
    the whole ancestor chain) - a category node's children are determined
    by the node itself, same reasoning already established for CONNECT's
    one-level source/target dependency. The CLI's own breadcrumb stack is
    for backing out visually, not something list_at needs to see.

  Rejected alternatives:
  - A dedicated new DataArgKind / a list_children-style call for tree
    enumeration - not a genuinely new interaction shape (still "pick from
    an enumerable list", just applied recursively), so a new contract
    surface would violate the "a new arg kind is the only thing that
    should touch the UI" rule the rest of the action system was built on.
  - Returning a nested tree from one list_at call - breaks the flat-
    pagination contract every existing caller relies on, and would need
    heap-allocated variable-depth structures crossing data_actions.h's
    dependency-free boundary. The flag-plus-drill-loop keeps everything
    flat and caller-stack-owned, matching "all interface buffers are
    caller stack" already established for the rest of this system.

  Checked for robustness against MULTI_CHOICE and CONNECT specifically
  (not just the single-list ADD_CHOICE case) before writing this down -
  category_scope as a top-level field (not a reused union field) is what
  makes it compose with CONNECT's existing source/target scoping without
  collision; see the correction note above.
